{"benchmark": "livebench_selected", "task_id": "paraphrase:91b1079c3dd6f0c003bb20f34f3783141cbc55926e6e3001ba18eaba3acc169e", "sample_idx": 12, "model_name": "qwen/qwen3-next-80b-a3b-instruct", "content": "\"skill in solving Sudoku puzzles demands focus and logic, much like navigating the complex weight of climate policy decisions. Just as each number from 1 to 9 must occupy a unique space in rows, columns, and boxes, so too must governments balance competing priorities without repetition or contradiction—ensuring no single interest overwhelms the system.\n\n\\n\\n\n\nThe temporary nature of many climate fixes—like carbon offsets or gas-powered emergency backups—echoes the fleeting satisfaction of a half-solved grid. These stopgaps may appear to fill gaps, but without a complete, coherent strategy, they leave the bigger picture unresolved. The puzzle’s integrity, like planetary stability, crumbles if corners are cut.\n\n\\n\\n\n\nA sudden twist in the weather patterns or energy markets can render even the most elegant plan obsolete. Just as a misplaced digit can cascade into grid failure, a surge in gas prices or a heatwave can destabilize fragile systems. Adaptability, not rigidity, becomes the true measure of resilience.\n\n\\n\\n\n\nThe weight of responsibility rests not just on policymakers but on every participant in the system—citizens, corporations, and institutions alike. Solving the climate puzzle requires the same discipline as completing a Sudoku: patience, precision, and the courage to revise assumptions. Only then can the full picture emerge—clear, balanced, and complete.\"", "logprobs": [{"token": "\"", "logprob": -2.4437606043647975e-05}, {"token": "skill", "logprob": -0.0022319662384688854}, {"token": " in", "logprob": -0.023988250643014908}, {"token": " solving", "logprob": -0.013891911134123802}, {"token": " Sudoku", "logprob": -1.2473725080490112}, {"token": " puzzles", "logprob": -0.20090025663375854}, {"token": " demands", "logprob": -1.14789998